Step 4 — Understand our core idea
Before writing the Factory, understand this.
Without Factory:

check_pii()
check_injection()
check_toxicity()
check_policy()
and somewhere else:
check_pii()
check_hallucination()
check_grounding()
and somewhere else:
check_authorization()
check_transaction()
This spreads policy decisions across the application.
Our approach becomes:

factory = GuardrailFactory()

pipeline = factory.create_pipeline(
    stage="input"
)
Then:
result = pipeline.execute(user_input)
The application doesn't need to know exactly which guardrails are inside.
That's the key architectural idea.

Step 5 — First module: Guardrail Result
Create:
app/
└── models/
    └── guardrail_result.py
Put this inside:
from pydantic import BaseModel


class GuardrailResult(BaseModel):
    allowed: bool
    guardrail: str
    reason: str = ""
    modified_input: str | None = None
Now we have a common contract.
Every guardrail will eventually return:

allowed
guardrail
reason
modified_input
For example:
allowed = False
guardrail = "PII"
reason = "Account number detected"
This becomes very important later because the Factory can treat every guardrail consistently.

Step 6 — Create the Guardrail interface
Create:
app/
└── guards/
    └── base_guard.py
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod
from app.models.guardrail_result import GuardrailResult


class BaseGuardrail(ABC):

    @property
    @abstractmethod
    def name(self) -> str:
        pass

    @abstractmethod
    def check(self, text: str) -> GuardrailResult:
        pass
Now every guardrail must implement:
name
check()
For example:
PIIGuard
InjectionGuard
ToxicityGuard
all follow the same contract.

Step 7 — Our first real guardrail
Create:
app/
└── guards/
    └── input/
        └── pii_guard.py
Start simple.
import re

from app.guards.base_guard import BaseGuardrail
from app.models.guardrail_result import GuardrailResult


class PIIGuard(BaseGuardrail):

    @property
    def name(self) -> str:
        return "PII"

    def check(self, text: str) -> GuardrailResult:

        account_pattern = r"\b\d{10,16}\b"

        if re.search(account_pattern, text):
            return GuardrailResult(
                allowed=False,
                guardrail=self.name,
                reason="Possible bank account number detected."
            )

        return GuardrailResult(
            allowed=True,
            guardrail=self.name
        )
This is deliberately simple.
We're learning the architecture first, not building a production PII detector yet.

Step 8 — Create Injection Guard
Create:
app/guards/input/injection_guard.py
from app.guards.base_guard import BaseGuardrail
from app.models.guardrail_result import GuardrailResult


class InjectionGuard(BaseGuardrail):

    @property
    def name(self) -> str:
        return "PromptInjection"

    def check(self, text: str) -> GuardrailResult:

        patterns = [
            "ignore previous instructions",
            "ignore all instructions",
            "reveal system prompt",
            "bypass safety"
        ]

        normalized = text.lower()

        for pattern in patterns:
            if pattern in normalized:
                return GuardrailResult(
                    allowed=False,
                    guardrail=self.name,
                    reason=f"Potential prompt injection detected: {pattern}"
                )

        return GuardrailResult(
            allowed=True,
            guardrail=self.name
        )
Again, later we'll make this more sophisticated.

Step 9 — NOW comes the important part
Create:
app/factory/guardrail_factory.py
This is the heart of your project.
from app.guards.input.pii_guard import PIIGuard
from app.guards.input.injection_guard import InjectionGuard


class GuardrailFactory:

    def create_input_guardrails(self):

        return [
            PIIGuard(),
            InjectionGuard()
        ]
Now your application doesn't do:
PIIGuard()
InjectionGuard()
everywhere.
Instead:

factory = GuardrailFactory()

guards = factory.create_input_guardrails()
That's your first Factory pattern.
Step 10 — Create the Input Pipeline
Create:
app/pipeline/input_pipeline.py
from app.factory.guardrail_factory import GuardrailFactory


class InputGuardrailPipeline:

    def __init__(self):

        factory = GuardrailFactory()

        self.guardrails = factory.create_input_guardrails()

    def execute(self, text: str):

        for guardrail in self.guardrails:

            result = guardrail.check(text)

            print(
                f"[{guardrail.name}] "
                f"{'PASS' if result.allowed else 'BLOCK'}"
            )

            if not result.allowed:
                return result

        return {
            "allowed": True,
            "message": "Input passed all guardrails."
        }
Notice what's happening.
The pipeline doesn't know:

PII
Injection
Toxicity
Policy
It only knows:
Guardrail
   ↓
check()
   ↓
Result
That's the abstraction we want.
